WEBVTT

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.460
What you're looking at is a bit of paper with holes in it.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.020
How's that for a spectacular way to start a program?

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:43.420
But this may be the most important bit of paper with holes in it since the hole was invented.

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:49.940
It's a punch card, and it stands for one of the most meaningful inventions man has ever given himself.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:50.970
The computer.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:56.580
In most people's everyday life, the computer isn't much more than a very fast adding machine.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:59.970
It tends to send you bills, but it is very...

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.280
much more than that. The modern world could not function without computers

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:07.680
because they operate everything from production lines to telephone exchanges

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:12.220
to traffic systems to international finance. But the main reason why

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:17.050
computers matter to you and me in our future is because they have perfect memories.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:22.530
They never forget anything they're told about you and me. The kind of data, say,

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:26.690
you have to give somebody if you want a bank account or credit or to be able to vote

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:29.420
or buy a house or if you've been accused of a crime.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:33.900
And that's why computers contain the future within them.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.710
If you tell a computer everything about a group of people,

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:41.020
it'll juggle the mix and come up with the one factor

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:44.550
that is most likely to affect the decision that group will make about something

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:45.880
one way or the other.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.570
Knowing that is knowing the future.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:51.920
And that is power.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:54.170
But in whose hand?

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:59.980
This computer has a particularly spectacular kind of memory of the past and knowledge of...

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.860
of the future in its databanks.

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:03.370
Watch this.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:06.330
This computer runs a planetarium and so it handles, as a matter of simple routine,

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.420
the universe as far forward in time as you like or back.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.320
How would you like the beginning of everything?

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:33.880
Or here's one full turn of the earth, tomorrow, in 15 seconds.

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:19.330
Now, the reason I'm showing you all this fun and games is not because I'm some kind of astronomy freak.

00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:25.950
It's because the very first time mankind gained the power to see into the future, like the computer can now,

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:29.820
that power was in the hands of the priest astronomers over...

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.750
3,000 years ago, and look what that led to.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:34.760
First, the moon.

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:39.710
Roughly every 12 times you saw this happen,

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:41.310
the waxing and waning of the moon,

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:44.320
you were back where you started in terms of planting

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:46.950
or harvesting or irrigation once a year.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:50.140
So the priest astronomers were able to tell the farmers,

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:51.260
plant now.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:53.680
Now, the more they looked up at the moon,

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:55.300
the more they learned about the stars.

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:57.610
And so by the fifth century BC,

00:04:58.200 --> 00:04:59.970
they had identified and...

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:07.340
named most of the major constellations 12 of them stretching across the sky aries

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:25.610
taurus gemini cancer leo virgo libra scorpio sagittarius capricorn aquarius and pisces

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:29.830
So they divided the sky into 12 sections, six...

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.210
in one hemisphere, six in the other.

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:34.520
They called these sections houses

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:38.150
and gave the houses power over life, health, money,

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:39.940
love, and so on.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:46.000
So which house a constellation was in

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.060
affected life on Earth, they said.

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:51.190
Now, the more this astrology stuff became popular,

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:52.650
the more the astronomers saw.

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:56.780
They saw, for instance, that besides the sun and the moon,

00:05:57.060 --> 00:05:59.300
there were five other things in the sky that moved.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:09.140
like this. So they called them the wanderers, in Greek, planeta, planets. So, by the second

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:14.320
century AD, this is the kind of stuff the astronomers were turning out. It's a copy

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:19.120
of a bit of an astronomical bestseller by a fellow called Claudius Ptolemy. It's a star

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:23.230
table. There's the name of the star, which constellation it's in, how many degrees up

00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:29.960
in the sky, which hemisphere, how bright. And he did that for 1,022 stars. But it was

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:31.780
still the old magic that got the crowds on their feet,

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:35.560
especially the magic of being able to predict something like this.

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:49.810
So, by the Middle Ages, they thought they got a pretty clear idea

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:51.070
of the kind of universe they got.

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:53.560
I say they, we didn't.

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:55.720
Up here in cold, windy Northern Europe,

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:58.800
we were too busy just trying to get through the Dark Ages in one piece.

00:06:59.050 --> 00:06:59.970
But the Arabs didn't.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.830
And they'd learnt it from the Persians and the Indians.

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:04.580
Take a look at this.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:10.340
This is a mechanical version of an Arabic universe we built

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:11.680
so we could crank it around.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:14.670
First, for very obvious reasons,

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:17.370
they thought the Earth, here where I am now,

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:19.210
was at the centre of everything.

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:26.930
All around it, out in space, was a gigantic crystal sphere

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:29.680
carrying the constellations and rotating.

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:37.320
Inside that were another seven crystal spheres, also rotating, each one carrying a different planet.

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:40.400
Closest to the Earth, they thought, was the Moon.

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:51.250
Then Mercury, then Venus, then came the Sun, and beyond that, Mars, and then Jupiter, and finally Saturn.

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:59.300
If you were an Arab, all this was vital because it told you in which direction Mecca, the Holy City, lay.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.320
And it told you what time it was so that you could pray when you should.

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:06.810
Because they had this, it's a kind of heavenly computer called an astrolabe.

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:11.250
Each one of these little points represents a star and you can move the whole lot so that

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:14.770
you can reproduce what you see in the sky on a particular night and when you've done that,

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:16.670
the astrolabe will tell you what the date is.

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:19.740
On the other side, there are these two little sights.

00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:27.050
You line the sights up on a star, then you read the angle off there, and you look that

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:29.970
angle up in your star tables and they tell you that the star will.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.320
be at that angle at a certain time of night, accurate to within a minute. And of course

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:37.590
if you know what direction the star is in, you can work out where Mecca is.

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:43.880
So the Arabs were able to say where everything in the sky would be throughout the year at

00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:50.920
any time, day or night. I mean, take for example Dawn on September the 14th, 714 AD. At that

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:55.670
point, Venus would have been up in the third house, let's get it up there. Mercury would

00:08:55.670 --> 00:08:59.180
have been in the second house, and everybody else would have been clustered in the first

00:08:59.180 --> 00:08:59.980
together with the

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.300
constellation Virgo except for Jupiter which would have been down below the

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:12.150
horizon in the 12th house. So since this vast universe was so powerful, I mean the

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:16.080
Sun made plants grow and the stars changed with the seasons, everybody

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:21.520
reckoned that the sky ruled all life on Earth. So if you got sick say, a great

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:26.940
deal hung on what was in the sky when you were born and that's why dawn on

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:29.750
September the 14th, 714 matters so much.

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:34.430
It was the birthday of the Caliph al-Mansur, the founder of Baghdad,

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:38.780
who in 765 got as sick as a dog and couldn't be cured.

00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:43.740
And what happened because of that is one of the most extraordinary accidents in history.

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:49.730
You see, up in the mountains a few hundred miles away, at a place called Jundi Shapur,

00:09:49.910 --> 00:09:52.030
there was a monastery that had a medical school.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:55.340
And finally, in desperation, the director of the medical school was invited down

00:09:55.340 --> 00:09:57.300
and he promptly cured the Caliph.

00:09:57.540 --> 00:09:59.860
And he probably used astrology to do it.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:02.840
See, astrologers reproduced what was in the sky on a chart,

00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:04.660
very much like the ones in your daily paper.

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:09.160
Each planet or constellation was supposed to have power

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:14.990
over metals, plants, medicine, your career, your life, disease,

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:16.910
you name it, they ruled.

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:20.620
So any medical astrologer looking at the Caliph's birth horoscope

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:22.740
would have said, wow, look at this lot here

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:24.600
in one influential grouping in the house of life.

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:26.980
I mean, Virgo gives you stomach trouble,

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:29.540
Saturn gives you a hernia, Mars gives you ulcers,

00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:29.940
and the

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:34.400
moon makes you vomit so diagnosis would have been a piece of cake. Now you may

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:40.020
think that's a lot of garbage, so do I, but history records that Al-Mansur actually

00:10:40.020 --> 00:10:45.530
did have stomach trouble. Well he was up and about in no time and Jundi Shapur

00:10:45.530 --> 00:10:49.760
became a very popular place and that's when it happened because when the Arabs

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:52.600
turned up for a look around they found it stuffed full of manuscripts

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:57.020
containing a vast amount of ancient Greek knowledge which they jumped at and

00:10:57.050 --> 00:10:57.790
and started translating.

00:10:58.690 --> 00:10:59.980
Knowledge that might...

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:05.290
been lost to us forever if Al-Mansur hadn't gone sick. Because as Arab civilization moved

00:11:05.290 --> 00:11:10.270
west across the Mediterranean, it took that knowledge with it, and a belief in a mechanical

00:11:10.270 --> 00:11:16.000
universe whose signs could be read for the benefit of mankind, trapped inside it.

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:29.980
New knowledge was to take us out.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:35.060
of the treadmill of ignorance and put us in charge of our lives in a way we'd never been before.

00:11:36.150 --> 00:11:41.310
Especially when the amazing new Arab translations of medical stuff got to pox-ridden Europe.

00:11:41.900 --> 00:11:48.200
The first European medical unit was set up at Salerno in Italy in the 11th century,

00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:59.960
complete with do-it-yourself instruction manuals like this lot. Salerno had its first big success as

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.620
a medieval mash, you know, crusaders stopping off on their way back for repairs, and then

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:09.500
moving on to tell the folks at home all about this amazing new thing called curing people.

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:12.460
Thank you.

00:12:13.460 --> 00:12:13.760
Will.

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:19.160
To the Europeans, so short of medical knowledge that with any disease you tended to get seriously dead.

00:12:19.770 --> 00:12:21.350
This was something they had to have.

00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:23.380
and in their new universities.

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:27.920
one of the two really avant-garde things to study became doctoring.

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:36.920
The other academic turn-on was also due to the Muslims.

00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:41.240
When the Christians took Toledo back from the Spanish Arabs in 1105,

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:44.360
they found libraries stacked with astronomy books,

00:12:44.460 --> 00:12:46.360
which the king promptly ordered translated.

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:49.910
You could hear the sound of scribbling and excitement all over Europe.

00:12:50.660 --> 00:12:52.280
Remember those star tables of Ptolemies?

00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:54.120
That sort of stuff was turning up.

00:12:55.020 --> 00:12:58.720
Well, by the 13th century, anybody studying the new medicine and astronomy

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:00.000
was into what we would call...

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.040
scientific investigation, a hard-nosed approach to things that definitely underwhelmed one

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:06.480
very heavy bunch of people.

00:13:15.230 --> 00:13:20.140
See, these were the days of the great clerical carve-up between the traditionalists in the

00:13:20.140 --> 00:13:23.400
church and the new thinkers who'd got turned on by the new science.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:31.640
Amen, secundum dialettica.

00:13:32.810 --> 00:13:35.690
On the one hand, the traditionalists who said,

00:13:35.790 --> 00:13:37.130
listen, all you have to do is believe,

00:13:37.170 --> 00:13:38.810
and whatever the early church fathers wrote,

00:13:38.950 --> 00:13:40.310
that's the horse's mouth.

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:44.270
And on the other...

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:48.240
the new thinkers who reckoned that if something didn't stand up to analysis and argument,

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:50.200
then you couldn't trust it no matter who it was.

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:54.300
Well, that kind of made the traditionalists foam at the mouth.

00:13:54.580 --> 00:13:57.280
To them, it was revolutionary claptrap.

00:13:57.420 --> 00:13:59.540
Though to us, this new view is obvious.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:03.960
If you could examine the sky and the human body and find out how they worked,

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:05.960
Was there any limit to what you could do?

00:14:06.620 --> 00:14:08.660
Why believe anything unless you could prove it?

00:14:09.270 --> 00:14:11.190
Our modern approach to learning starts here.

00:14:12.090 --> 00:14:18.830
But what drove the traditionalists' bananas was that these guys were even suggesting you could apply the same experimental approach.

00:14:19.580 --> 00:14:20.660
to what God was.

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:24.640
Per intellectus defectum, cogantur descendere.

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:27.660
Sede pedrus appelardus, magister nostre.

00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:29.140
Worst of all, they said this.

00:14:29.320 --> 00:14:29.960
You compare.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.620
one holy writer with another on some crucial point,

00:14:33.190 --> 00:14:33.950
And they don't agree.

00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:37.240
Scrittore un vizio corrutta sun!

00:14:38.220 --> 00:14:41.300
Put yourself in the Pope's position with all these guys rocking the boat.

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:42.840
There was only one thing to do.

00:14:43.240 --> 00:14:44.340
Invent the Inquisition.

00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:46.240
And we all know what that got up to.

00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:47.860
Very nasty stuff.

00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:49.020
Neil Glarus.

00:15:00.700 --> 00:15:00.980
Now,

00:15:01.980 --> 00:15:02.540
Wow

00:15:02.940 --> 00:15:03.920
Some of the...

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:07.730
knowledge coming in via the Arabs was causing a church a load of trouble.

00:15:08.440 --> 00:15:12.220
Some of it was about to help solve a really serious problem.

00:15:13.050 --> 00:15:15.070
The problem the church had was telling the time.

00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:16.440
You see,

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:21.530
The church had rules that if you were a monk, you had to say certain prayers at certain hours of the day and night,

00:15:21.770 --> 00:15:26.510
and as a monk, your salvation depended on doing that properly, so knowing what time it was was vital.

00:15:27.580 --> 00:15:27.800
Now,

00:15:28.120 --> 00:15:29.460
In the Mediterranean, in the south,

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:31.020
They had sundials.

00:15:31.620 --> 00:15:33.340
And they had candles. Now, they had both.

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:37.550
up here in Northern Europe too, but the problem with the sundial in Northern Europe is that...

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:38.660
There's not very much sun.

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:40.260
It's cloudy a lot of the time.

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:43.260
And as for candles, well, at the time, they were very expensive.

00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:46.320
and they did sometimes tend to burn places down.

00:15:47.270 --> 00:15:51.030
Now the only way we know how they solved the problem of getting the prayer...

00:15:51.270 --> 00:15:56.390
write at the right time was because of a chronicle written by a monk called Jocelyn de Bracalonde.

00:15:57.160 --> 00:15:59.840
And he lived in the Abbey Church of Bury St Edmunds.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.280
in England, where the body of St Edmund was buried.

00:16:02.420 --> 00:16:03.640
So it was a very important church.

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:07.860
Anyway, he says that one night, June the 23rd, 1198,

00:16:08.920 --> 00:16:10.460
everybody in the abbey was awakened

00:16:10.460 --> 00:16:13.060
by the sound of the most gigantic fire.

00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:15.540
And they raced out, terrified, and sure enough,

00:16:15.620 --> 00:16:16.680
the whole place was going up in smoke.

00:16:16.780 --> 00:16:17.700
It was a major catastrophe.

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:20.420
And he says, the young men among us ran,

00:16:20.540 --> 00:16:23.250
some to the well and some to the clock.

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:27.340
Now, the reason they ran to a clock to put the fire out

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:29.340
was because they were using a water clock.

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:34.520
And that's fine by day, but who wakes you up at night and says,

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:36.760
excuse me, it's five o'clock and you've got to do that particular prayer?

00:16:37.460 --> 00:16:41.220
Now, we haven't known for centuries how they solved the problem of waking up in the middle of the night.

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:48.210
Until recently, when a fragment of an 11th century manuscript was discovered in a monastery in the Pyrenees.

00:16:48.450 --> 00:16:53.870
And in this fragment, it said in Latin, of course, this is how you build a water-powered alarm clock.

00:16:54.700 --> 00:16:57.460
So we did. We think it's the only one in the world.

00:16:58.060 --> 00:16:59.980
And you know what? It worked.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:05.850
I think it's fantastic. Here's the reservoir of water. Running water coming

00:17:05.850 --> 00:17:10.490
down here at a very carefully controlled speed fills the reservoir here. There's a

00:17:10.490 --> 00:17:14.230
float down in there and on top of the float is this shaft and on the shaft

00:17:14.230 --> 00:17:18.130
there are teeth. Now as the float rises slowly these teeth engage in this cog

00:17:18.130 --> 00:17:22.910
system here on the face of the clock and that turns the clock very slowly and

00:17:22.910 --> 00:17:26.350
for daytime there's your clock you know what time it is. Now for your alarm call

00:17:26.350 --> 00:17:29.750
at night, you take a little sliver of metal like that...

00:17:30.190 --> 00:17:33.210
and you stick it in one of these slits around the outside of the clock face,

00:17:33.310 --> 00:17:35.470
depending what time you want, like, say, 3 o'clock in the morning.

00:17:35.810 --> 00:17:37.670
And when 3 o'clock in the morning comes around,

00:17:37.910 --> 00:17:42.370
that little metal sliver rises just and touches this ledge here

00:17:42.370 --> 00:17:44.110
and trips it as it goes by,

00:17:44.330 --> 00:17:47.090
causing this tiny weight to swing down here,

00:17:47.380 --> 00:17:50.280
pulling this noose off that restraining arm.

00:17:50.520 --> 00:17:52.320
Now, once the noose is off that restraining arm,

00:17:52.480 --> 00:17:54.380
this shaft here spins round and round.

00:17:54.620 --> 00:17:56.700
Now, it does that because in here,

00:17:56.840 --> 00:17:58.700
it's got rope wound round and round it,

00:17:58.740 --> 00:17:59.980
and that rope's attached to some very heavy...

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:05.400
weights so when the shaft is freed the weights fall and the shaft spins and when it does this

00:18:05.400 --> 00:18:10.500
bit here goes forward and hits the bell and that's your alarm call and who said genius was simple

00:18:10.500 --> 00:18:11.970
look at it in action

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:49.950
Showbiz. But the trouble was in winter the water still froze. So it wasn't until

00:18:49.950 --> 00:18:54.450
about 1280 that somebody came up with a brilliant idea of getting rid of the

00:18:54.450 --> 00:18:58.280
water completely and using only the weights to power the clock. The system

00:18:58.280 --> 00:18:59.620
that made that idea feasible.

00:19:00.280 --> 00:19:03.380
was probably one of the greatest inventions in the history of mankind,

00:19:04.420 --> 00:19:06.130
the verge and foliate.

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:15.440
This is it.

00:19:15.970 --> 00:19:17.470
You see this wheel with teeth in it?

00:19:17.570 --> 00:19:22.380
Well, it's being turned by weight and a piece of rope wrapped around a shaft,

00:19:22.500 --> 00:19:23.780
just like the one in the alarm clock.

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:25.900
It's not whizzing around so fast

00:19:25.900 --> 00:19:29.340
because it's being held in check by the verge and foliate system I told you about.

00:19:29.440 --> 00:19:29.600
Look.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:33.500
This is the verge bit and it has a blade there which comes in and catches the teeth

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:35.940
and stops the wheel moving for a second like that.

00:19:36.430 --> 00:19:39.360
Now, every time it does that, it's then kicked away by the wheel

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:41.000
because the wheel's being pulled by the main weight.

00:19:41.200 --> 00:19:45.060
And when that happens, another blade down at the bottom catches in the teeth

00:19:45.060 --> 00:19:48.990
and holds the wheel for another split second before it, too, is kicked away.

00:19:49.490 --> 00:19:53.190
And the two blades alternate between them, back and forth, holding the wheel back all the time.

00:19:53.380 --> 00:19:59.960
And as they operate, you can hear the sound with which the world has become familiar over the last 700 years.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:21.050
years, the sound of tick-tock, tick-tock. By 1400, what had started as a machine to tell

00:20:21.050 --> 00:20:25.040
people when to pray had become a machine for telling people when to work.

00:20:44.970 --> 00:20:48.190
Clowns all over Europe got clocks, and when they rang, you moved.

00:20:48.330 --> 00:20:51.290
And you moved until they rang again when you stopped.

00:20:51.930 --> 00:20:53.010
Does that idea ring a bell?

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:04.750
This was also when we got our 12-hour clock because striking 24 for one of these monsters

00:21:04.750 --> 00:21:05.850
was much too complicated.

00:21:09.040 --> 00:21:12.130
The new clocks made the towns more efficient and production went up.

00:21:13.450 --> 00:21:19.970
Okay, put yourself back then, say in the position of a successful merchant or businessman, riding

00:21:19.970 --> 00:21:22.310
high on the late medieval economic boom.

00:21:23.140 --> 00:21:27.580
All around you, towns are running their businesses with clocks like these in their towers.

00:21:28.420 --> 00:21:29.980
Time is becoming money.

00:21:30.440 --> 00:21:31.940
So what do you want? You want time.

00:21:32.480 --> 00:21:34.160
But you don't want it like that up a tower.

00:21:34.320 --> 00:21:35.120
You want it in your pocket.

00:21:36.090 --> 00:21:37.830
So there must have been a very ready market around

00:21:37.830 --> 00:21:41.360
when some craftsman, I don't know, around about 1450, 1460,

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:42.940
made the next great step.

00:21:43.890 --> 00:21:45.950
He might even have been, say, a locksmith.

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:48.400
Because if you look at the locks of the time,

00:21:48.620 --> 00:21:51.180
you see that they work, they stay open or stay closed,

00:21:51.430 --> 00:21:54.200
because of the power of that bending metal.

00:21:55.680 --> 00:21:57.400
So the spring is the concept I'm talking about.

00:21:58.070 --> 00:21:59.890
But it was what you do with the spring.

00:22:00.120 --> 00:22:05.220
that was really clever. You see, a spring has problems. When it's wound up tight, it

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:09.360
starts to unwind, it's working very powerfully, strongly. As it unwinds, it becomes weaker

00:22:09.360 --> 00:22:13.920
and weaker. So if you're going to get a spring to work, you have to make sure that it's given

00:22:13.920 --> 00:22:19.460
the hard work at the beginning and the easy work at the end. Now, in clocks, this is how

00:22:19.460 --> 00:22:24.750
they did that. They put the spring inside a barrel, there's a barrel, and the spring

00:22:24.750 --> 00:22:28.830
is connected to the barrel so that as the spring unwinds, the barrel turns. Now, coiled

00:22:28.830 --> 00:22:29.970
around the outside of the back.

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:32.840
there's either cat-gut or chain in this case,

00:22:33.020 --> 00:22:34.900
and that chain is then taken across over here

00:22:34.900 --> 00:22:38.160
and wound round and round the main drive shaft of the clock.

00:22:38.800 --> 00:22:41.690
But you see that? That drive shaft is tapered,

00:22:41.790 --> 00:22:45.690
so that as the chain unwinds, the first parts unwind down here,

00:22:45.890 --> 00:22:47.810
where the hard work has to be done by the spring,

00:22:47.970 --> 00:22:52.130
and then as it weakens, it's unwinding further and further up the shaft

00:22:52.130 --> 00:22:54.530
until right at the top the easiest work is being done,

00:22:54.590 --> 00:22:55.970
the position it's in now, as a matter of fact.

00:22:56.870 --> 00:22:59.250
Now, that threaded shaft there is called a fusee.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:03.300
And if you're ever looking at your wristwatch one day and wondering why you're a slave to it,

00:23:03.400 --> 00:23:05.060
blame the fusee. That's where it all started.

00:23:06.350 --> 00:23:10.460
These spring-driven clocks were, as you would expect, instant raving success.

00:23:10.840 --> 00:23:13.900
I mean, imagine being given something like that

00:23:13.900 --> 00:23:20.770
when the world you live in is full of bears and wolves and dark forests and muddy tracks and thatched ruins.

00:23:21.550 --> 00:23:25.370
It must have been like one of us getting a personal interstellar spacecraft to play with

00:23:25.370 --> 00:23:26.610
and park around behind the house.

00:23:28.900 --> 00:23:29.980
Most of these...

00:23:30.060 --> 00:23:35.340
medieval mechanical marvels came from the same town in southern Germany that this one did

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:58.930
because it's called a Nuremberg egg. Okay, enough of the tourist bit. On with the story.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:03.840
was good at clock making because it was good at metal work because it was in the

00:24:03.840 --> 00:24:10.010
greatest mining area in Europe gold silver iron copper so it was very very

00:24:10.010 --> 00:24:14.800
rich and it had the best craftsmen in Europe they used to boast that your

00:24:14.800 --> 00:24:19.290
average burger here lived better than the king of Scotland Nuremberg was also

00:24:19.290 --> 00:24:23.890
on a main road so a lot of people came through in most cases they came for the

00:24:23.890 --> 00:24:27.870
expertise that the craftsmen here guarded so jealously keeping their

00:24:27.870 --> 00:24:29.910
earnings up by keeping other craftsmen under

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:36.050
At one end of the scale, they produced some of the best armour around.

00:24:36.460 --> 00:24:41.040
At the other, extremely delicate and extremely expensive trinkets like this.

00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:44.740
Nuremberg became a centre for research into metallurgy,

00:24:44.820 --> 00:24:46.560
and since they also ran silver mines,

00:24:46.680 --> 00:24:48.980
they also had the biggest banking corporations in Europe.

00:24:49.260 --> 00:24:51.820
They lent money to everybody to finance their wars,

00:24:52.060 --> 00:24:53.540
and then made even more money

00:24:53.540 --> 00:24:56.380
by selling them a nice little line in weapons they also made.

00:24:56.970 --> 00:24:58.810
And where have we heard that trick before?

00:24:58.810 --> 00:24:59.940
But-

00:25:00.120 --> 00:25:01.720
The point of all this, I hear you ask?

00:25:02.080 --> 00:25:05.020
Well, because Nuremberg was so well-placed

00:25:05.020 --> 00:25:07.540
to make fantastically complex clocks like this,

00:25:07.880 --> 00:25:10.700
it started turning out instruments that were just as accurate,

00:25:11.330 --> 00:25:12.550
sky-watching instruments.

00:25:13.720 --> 00:25:15.540
Thanks to the locksmiths and the clockmakers,

00:25:15.760 --> 00:25:17.920
you could now look at the sky in such detail

00:25:17.920 --> 00:25:19.320
with the new improved instruments,

00:25:19.560 --> 00:25:22.320
you began to see that there was something wrong

00:25:22.320 --> 00:25:25.470
with the old idea of the Earth being at the centre of everything.

00:25:29.440 --> 00:25:29.960
This...

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:35.400
possibility took Europe by storm. Up north, everybody was talking about it. Down south,

00:25:35.640 --> 00:25:41.400
here in Italy, nobody was. Or if they were, they made sure they were alone. Well, most

00:25:41.400 --> 00:25:46.230
of them did, except one, which is why our story takes us next to the Italian cathedral

00:25:46.230 --> 00:25:53.310
city of Pisa. And this is where the Catholic Church comes into the picture. It was probably

00:25:53.310 --> 00:25:58.610
a man called Hans Lipperske, a spectacle maker who lived in Holland, who is as responsible

00:25:58.610 --> 00:25:59.490
as anybody.

00:26:00.250 --> 00:26:01.390
for kicking the whole thing off.

00:26:01.600 --> 00:26:05.480
Because in 1603, he offered this to the Dutch army

00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:06.380
for use in the battlefield.

00:26:07.140 --> 00:26:08.280
He called it his looker.

00:26:08.790 --> 00:26:10.800
We call it a telescope.

00:26:12.010 --> 00:26:15.610
A year later, in 1609, one was in the hands of Galileo.

00:26:15.990 --> 00:26:18.230
And from the very first moment that he looked through it

00:26:18.230 --> 00:26:20.790
at the night sky, our view of where we stand

00:26:20.790 --> 00:26:21.910
in the universe changed.

00:26:22.660 --> 00:26:25.750
Because what Galileo saw confirmed what the Polish

00:26:25.750 --> 00:26:27.730
astronomer Copernicus had been saying theoretically

00:26:27.730 --> 00:26:29.970
for 60 years, that the Earth

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:33.290
was not the centre of the universe with everything going round it.

00:26:37.760 --> 00:26:40.920
Galileo saw something that shook him rigid, Jupiter.

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:43.780
That was okay, but with moons going round it?

00:26:44.370 --> 00:26:46.690
So if Jupiter was the centre for its moons,

00:26:47.150 --> 00:26:49.750
that blew the idea that the Earth was the centre of everything.

00:26:53.220 --> 00:26:54.960
When Galileo published what he thought,

00:26:55.520 --> 00:26:59.050
the result was the Inquisition and house arrest until the day he died.

00:27:00.220 --> 00:27:03.500
In those days, you simply did not question what the Catholic Church said,

00:27:04.070 --> 00:27:07.380
and the Catholic Church said that the Earth was the centre of everything.

00:27:11.180 --> 00:27:14.920
However, something else Galileo wrote about wasn't quite so revolutionary,

00:27:15.160 --> 00:27:17.760
so it didn't get suppressed, so people got to hear about it.

00:27:17.980 --> 00:27:22.980
It was something he noticed that was to turn out to be the solution

00:27:22.980 --> 00:27:25.900
to another problem that the telescope created.

00:27:26.400 --> 00:27:29.660
You see, now astronomers had these things to observe with.

00:27:30.090 --> 00:27:35.150
They needed better clocks than they had to time the movements of the planets they were watching.

00:27:36.060 --> 00:27:40.190
And over a long period of time, a spring-driven clock was just not good enough.

00:27:42.130 --> 00:27:49.220
According to the legend, this was what Galileo noticed here in Pisa Cathedral one day in 1581.

00:27:50.470 --> 00:27:53.780
Out of curiosity, he timed the length of the swing on his pulse,

00:27:53.780 --> 00:27:58.690
and he noticed that the length of time it took the lamp to swing was exactly the same,

00:27:59.470 --> 00:27:59.970
whether it was a...

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:00.460
Big swing.

00:28:00.920 --> 00:28:02.120
Or as the swing listened...

00:28:02.260 --> 00:28:02.960
A little swing.

00:28:03.700 --> 00:28:04.740
In other words...

00:28:04.820 --> 00:28:07.640
It was an extremely accurate unit of measurement.

00:28:10.230 --> 00:28:15.430
Well, neither in Pisa nor anywhere else in Italy did Galileo do very much about his observation.

00:28:16.120 --> 00:28:19.860
It took somebody from a northern country, a Protestant country, with no fear of the Inquisition.

00:28:20.280 --> 00:28:22.060
to take things to their logical conclusion.

00:28:23.640 --> 00:28:26.960
He was an astronomer too and he needed an accurate clock just like everybody else.

00:28:27.480 --> 00:28:29.860
His name is Christian Huygens, a Dutchman.

00:28:30.340 --> 00:28:31.560
and in 1656.

00:28:32.230 --> 00:28:33.130
He produced this.

00:28:35.210 --> 00:28:35.690
a pendulum.

00:28:36.780 --> 00:28:39.440
You remember the virgin foliate system, the very first?

00:28:39.780 --> 00:28:40.960
mechanical clock system.

00:28:41.510 --> 00:28:42.490
The one that had...

00:28:42.700 --> 00:28:45.000
a big weight pulling a wheel with teeth on it,

00:28:45.360 --> 00:28:49.100
and the teeth were held in check one at a time by a rod with blades that went like this.

00:28:49.450 --> 00:28:50.610
as the wheel was pulled around.

00:28:51.620 --> 00:28:52.840
Well, instead of using

00:28:53.290 --> 00:28:56.490
a swinging weight to give those blades the power to hold the wheel.

00:28:57.030 --> 00:28:57.510
Huygens.

00:28:57.800 --> 00:28:59.240
use the swinging power of the pendulum.

00:29:00.310 --> 00:29:02.970
so that as the pendulum swung, the blades went hold.

00:29:03.320 --> 00:29:03.780
Release.

00:29:04.220 --> 00:29:05.140
Hold. Release.

00:29:05.780 --> 00:29:06.640
Hold. Release.

00:29:07.700 --> 00:29:08.680
Very accurate indeed.

00:29:09.140 --> 00:29:09.920
So it's solved.

00:29:10.200 --> 00:29:11.580
all the problems the astronomers had.

00:29:12.380 --> 00:29:15.480
But there was another bunch of stargazers who couldn't use that system.

00:29:16.060 --> 00:29:17.800
They were sailors who had to know the time.

00:29:18.490 --> 00:29:20.210
And the reason they couldn't use this system...

00:29:20.380 --> 00:29:20.860
Well...

00:29:21.110 --> 00:29:21.650
You try.

00:29:22.070 --> 00:29:23.650
a pendulum clock and a pitching C.

00:29:24.250 --> 00:29:24.990
and you'll get the point.

00:29:25.460 --> 00:29:26.760
the pendulum goes all over the place.

00:29:27.700 --> 00:29:28.680
Thank you.

00:29:37.870 --> 00:29:40.670
Now, the reason why navigators had to be able to tell the time

00:29:40.670 --> 00:29:44.800
was that by the beginning of the 17th century, the trade routes...

00:29:45.030 --> 00:29:47.770
to America and to India had been opened up.

00:29:47.890 --> 00:29:50.140
And the great maritime empires were really going,

00:29:50.300 --> 00:29:52.240
the Portuguese, the Spanish, the Dutch, and the English.

00:29:52.440 --> 00:29:55.210
And in that order, their governments offered

00:29:55.210 --> 00:29:57.190
really big money for anybody who would come up

00:29:57.190 --> 00:29:59.190
with a way to help their navigators till the time.

00:30:00.060 --> 00:30:00.580
Here's why.

00:30:01.530 --> 00:30:04.670
Each navigator had a book of star tables with him,

00:30:04.870 --> 00:30:09.330
and what those star tables told him was the position of any star or the moon.

00:30:10.260 --> 00:30:13.460
at any particular hour of any particular day during the year.

00:30:14.230 --> 00:30:14.910
And if he...

00:30:15.130 --> 00:30:17.110
observed a star, he would check...

00:30:17.240 --> 00:30:19.500
The position of it in the sky, so many degrees up.

00:30:19.860 --> 00:30:21.340
so many degrees to north or south.

00:30:21.880 --> 00:30:23.960
then he'd go and look in his book, and the book would say,

00:30:24.020 --> 00:30:25.700
well, if that's where it is at this particular time,

00:30:25.800 --> 00:30:28.580
then that means you're in the only place where you would see it in that position,

00:30:28.780 --> 00:30:29.980
therefore you must be at X.

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:31.070
And that would be his position.

00:30:32.310 --> 00:30:35.340
Now, the problem with that is that those star tables were only made

00:30:35.340 --> 00:30:39.670
to be used when you were going either north or south in the same time zone.

00:30:40.820 --> 00:30:44.670
Suppose you, say, go out into the Atlantic west towards America.

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:48.460
say to a point where the sun comes up an hour later than when you left home

00:30:48.460 --> 00:30:50.720
then all your measurements are an hour out.

00:30:50.920 --> 00:30:53.680
So you look at your star tables and the star tables tell you information

00:30:54.080 --> 00:30:57.780
that is okay for when you left home but not for where it's actually an hour later.

00:30:57.980 --> 00:30:59.980
And in terms of distance...

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:03.960
the Earth turns 1,000 miles during an hour.

00:31:04.380 --> 00:31:06.420
So your position is 1,000 miles out.

00:31:07.230 --> 00:31:09.290
And even if you got the time right to within one minute,

00:31:09.530 --> 00:31:11.230
you'd still be 15 miles off course.

00:31:11.670 --> 00:31:13.670
15 miles off course, you miss the place you're going to,

00:31:13.770 --> 00:31:15.590
you don't get the cargo you've been sent to pick up.

00:31:15.900 --> 00:31:17.580
And that kind of thing was happening all the time.

00:31:18.050 --> 00:31:21.330
They were absolutely desperate to get a clock to those navigators

00:31:21.330 --> 00:31:23.430
that would tell them what time it was back home,

00:31:23.590 --> 00:31:26.450
so they could make the necessary alterations in their star tables.

00:31:26.920 --> 00:31:29.680
And the one thing it couldn't be was a pendulum clock.

00:31:36.710 --> 00:31:37.690
So, where have we got?

00:31:38.220 --> 00:31:42.180
The Sikh caliph, the Arab science that turned on European monks.

00:31:42.660 --> 00:31:44.060
then need for alarm clocks,

00:31:44.520 --> 00:31:45.360
Up came the spring.

00:31:45.900 --> 00:31:49.880
Fancy metalwork followed and sky-watching instruments like the telescope...

00:31:50.220 --> 00:31:54.220
and then the pendulum clock that timed the planets, but was useless for navigators.

00:31:56.040 --> 00:31:59.860
Their need to get the position of the moon in the sky or a star right.

00:32:00.380 --> 00:32:07.030
Demanded a better clock spring and that need triggered off a series of events that begins with a spring but ends typically

00:32:07.030 --> 00:32:13.440
Somewhere different it starts of all places here in a glass making furnace in Sheffield

00:32:13.440 --> 00:32:16.720
And it ends with something that was good for the muscles

00:32:16.720 --> 00:32:24.000
Thanks to the way all these bits and pieces on this table go together you see the steel in the springs

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:29.860
Was made by piling up alternate layers of iron bars and charcoal and keeping the pile hot for

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:35.860
or anything up to a week, the carbon in the charcoal diffused into the surface of the iron

00:32:35.860 --> 00:32:40.420
and made a layer of steel. Now you knock those layers off and then you hammer them together

00:32:40.420 --> 00:32:45.480
and what you come up with is laminated steel made up of the compressed layers. Now when you coil

00:32:45.480 --> 00:32:50.030
that steel into a spring, you've given yourself a problem. Because of the different qualities of

00:32:50.030 --> 00:32:56.140
different layers, the spring tends to unwind irregularly. Now if only you could melt the

00:32:56.140 --> 00:32:59.960
layers, they would all run together and form a steel of uniform strength. But in

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:06.450
1740, who could melt steel? Well, the glass makers who worked in this place could, only

00:33:06.450 --> 00:33:12.490
they didn't know it. Until a clock maker called Benjamin Huntsman came along and noticed that

00:33:12.490 --> 00:33:17.970
the local glass makers were putting old bits of glass into their furnaces and managing

00:33:17.970 --> 00:33:23.230
to melt them because they lined the walls of these places with a kind of clay that reflected

00:33:23.230 --> 00:33:29.170
the heat back into the furnace. And that sent the temperature way up. The first thing Huntsman

00:33:29.170 --> 00:33:29.830
dead was to make

00:33:30.030 --> 00:33:34.870
pots out of that clay because it could stand terrific heat. Then he put these pots into

00:33:34.870 --> 00:33:39.190
one of the amazing new coke furnaces. And here's where he pinched the glassmaker's technique.

00:33:40.090 --> 00:33:45.700
He put bits of old steel into the pots, left the lot to simmer at very high temperature,

00:33:46.410 --> 00:33:52.200
and after five hours instead of a week, the pots came out of the furnace full of white

00:33:52.200 --> 00:33:58.100
hot melted steel that was just the stuff for clock springs. And, as it turned out, a lot

00:33:58.100 --> 00:33:58.600
of other things.

00:34:06.520 --> 00:34:10.280
So, the glass industry helped solve Huntsman's problem.

00:34:11.520 --> 00:34:12.860
The new steel also, incidentally,

00:34:13.020 --> 00:34:16.500
gave Sheffield steel cutlery its start in life,

00:34:16.790 --> 00:34:18.940
and that's a clue to what else it did too,

00:34:19.550 --> 00:34:22.310
because at the time, navigation instruments

00:34:22.310 --> 00:34:23.970
had become really very precise.

00:34:24.310 --> 00:34:25.490
Look at this sextant.

00:34:25.730 --> 00:34:26.970
This is the viewing tube.

00:34:27.070 --> 00:34:28.390
You put your eye at that end and you look,

00:34:28.450 --> 00:34:29.230
let's say, for the moon.

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:31.960
At the other end, half of it's glass,

00:34:32.080 --> 00:34:33.080
and you see the moon through that.

00:34:33.260 --> 00:34:34.720
The other half of it's a little mirror.

00:34:34.960 --> 00:34:36.700
And you also see the moon reflected in there

00:34:36.700 --> 00:34:39.250
because of this main reflecting mirror here.

00:34:39.700 --> 00:34:40.840
Picks up the image of the moon,

00:34:41.200 --> 00:34:42.340
sends it down to the tiny mirror,

00:34:42.420 --> 00:34:43.520
and back into your eye.

00:34:43.840 --> 00:34:45.500
So that when you look at the moon,

00:34:46.020 --> 00:34:47.240
you actually see two moons,

00:34:47.840 --> 00:34:49.480
one's almost superimposed on the other.

00:34:50.180 --> 00:34:52.100
To find how high the moon is in the sky,

00:34:52.520 --> 00:34:54.920
you come down until you're looking at the horizon,

00:34:55.440 --> 00:34:56.800
and then using this rod,

00:34:57.000 --> 00:34:59.300
you tilt the main reflecting mirror back.

00:35:00.030 --> 00:35:04.400
gradually until it picks up the moon again and now you see a horizon

00:35:04.400 --> 00:35:08.770
and a moon. The angle at which you had to tilt that main mirror back

00:35:08.770 --> 00:35:13.870
to find the moon is the angle at which the moon is up in the sky. It's a very precise instrument

00:35:13.870 --> 00:35:18.670
but it is only as precise as those scales down there off which you read the angle

00:35:19.180 --> 00:35:26.820
and that's where he comes in, Jesse Ramsden. Jesse Ramsden was the fellow who invented

00:35:26.820 --> 00:35:29.980
or stole a way of marking a scratch every six...

00:35:30.120 --> 00:35:37.530
of a degree on a sextant and he did it with a screw see how the foot pedal pulls a cord wound

00:35:37.530 --> 00:35:42.910
around the screw that turns the screw an exact number of times moving a big flat circular plate

00:35:42.910 --> 00:35:48.570
round exactly a sixth of a degree each time you mark the instrument clamped on top of the plate

00:35:49.650 --> 00:35:56.200
Ramsden's machine came out in 1774 cut the price of marking instruments by 50 times

00:35:56.200 --> 00:35:59.970
won him a prize and put him on the road to Richmond.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:07.380
This was the secret of Ramzen's success, the idea of using a screw to measure like that.

00:36:08.050 --> 00:36:13.140
But you could only get a good screw when you wanted one, thanks to what was going on in the furniture business.

00:36:14.620 --> 00:36:17.920
You know all that knobbly 17th century furniture with bulbous legs?

00:36:18.140 --> 00:36:23.200
Well that happened because of some unknown woodturner doing things to this, the pole lathe.

00:36:23.780 --> 00:36:29.030
Here's the springy pole, fix a strap to it, wind it round the wood and cut on every other turn.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:35.040
Now, if you give the turner something to rest his knife on that's the same shape as the leg you want,

00:36:35.240 --> 00:36:36.860
he can cut it to a pattern.

00:36:41.080 --> 00:36:45.080
Jesse Ramsden realised that if you cut a table leg by following a table leg pattern,

00:36:45.320 --> 00:36:47.800
then you should cut a screw by following a screw pattern.

00:36:48.320 --> 00:36:49.900
So this, at least in principle, is what he did.

00:36:50.370 --> 00:36:53.710
He mounted a cutter on a base that had a threaded hole in it,

00:36:53.790 --> 00:36:57.670
and through the threaded hole he put the master screw he wanted to copy,

00:36:57.950 --> 00:36:59.950
so that as he turned the master screw...

00:37:00.120 --> 00:37:01.360
the cutter moved along it.

00:37:01.760 --> 00:37:04.880
Now, with the workpiece turning on the lathe

00:37:04.880 --> 00:37:06.640
at exactly the same speed as the master screw,

00:37:07.260 --> 00:37:08.580
the cutter made an exact copy.

00:37:09.460 --> 00:37:11.540
All Ramzen had to find was a cutter

00:37:11.540 --> 00:37:14.100
that would make screw after screw without going blunt.

00:37:14.730 --> 00:37:16.890
And there was only one metal around

00:37:16.890 --> 00:37:19.590
that was remotely hard enough to do that job.

00:37:20.330 --> 00:37:21.710
Yes, Huntsman Steel.

00:37:24.950 --> 00:37:27.970
So, by 1797, all the ingredients in this story

00:37:27.970 --> 00:37:29.970
I've just told you were waiting around...

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:35.280
as so often happens in history for somebody to put them together which of course somebody did

00:37:35.280 --> 00:37:40.720
and this is another one of those rare times when somebody sees beyond the bits and pieces

00:37:40.720 --> 00:37:47.350
in 1800 Henry Maudsley put the edge of Huntsman steel together with Ramsden's idea of using the

00:37:47.350 --> 00:37:52.330
screw to measure with and came up with a machine that would cut metal to within a ten thousandth

00:37:52.330 --> 00:37:59.870
of an inch because he was a precision freak and he realized a screw that accurate would guide a cutter

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:01.380
to make anything else that accurate.

00:38:02.740 --> 00:38:05.300
And curiously, that was great news for the Navy.

00:38:13.520 --> 00:38:16.640
You see, at the time, boats used things called blocks

00:38:16.640 --> 00:38:19.310
by the thousand, complicated bits of wood

00:38:19.310 --> 00:38:20.470
with pulley wheels inside.

00:38:20.790 --> 00:38:22.480
Great for sailors with no muscles,

00:38:22.750 --> 00:38:25.310
because it gives you a pull three times your own strength.

00:38:30.960 --> 00:38:35.360
In 1800, a fellow called Brunel got Maudsley to use his precision machinery

00:38:35.360 --> 00:38:38.060
to make other machines that would make blocks.

00:38:38.600 --> 00:38:40.120
The British Navy jumped at the idea.

00:38:40.720 --> 00:38:44.480
By 1808, in Portsmouth Dockyard, this was happening.

00:38:44.900 --> 00:38:51.040
It may look like a bunch of dusty old guys cutting wood to you,

00:38:51.340 --> 00:38:54.560
but this is the beginnings of an idea that runs your life today.

00:38:57.250 --> 00:38:59.970
Maudsley built a long line of machines, each of which...

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:05.000
did one job. From a tree trunk the wood was sawn into wooden cubes and as it

00:39:05.000 --> 00:39:09.040
passed from one machine to the next the block was gradually shaped and drilled

00:39:09.040 --> 00:39:13.820
and contoured one step at a time and so were the wooden pulley wheels that fitted

00:39:13.820 --> 00:39:21.020
inside it. It took 43 separate machines to finish the job and 43 men each doing

00:39:21.020 --> 00:39:25.680
just one job over and over again. Sound familiar?

00:39:34.060 --> 00:39:40.570
With Maudsley's line of machines, each one doing just part of the operation,

00:39:41.120 --> 00:39:44.720
you've got yourself the ability to shove raw material in at one end of the line,

00:39:45.350 --> 00:39:48.650
run the whole thing from one power shaft spinning up above,

00:39:49.300 --> 00:39:52.520
and pick up the finished product as it comes off the last machine.

00:39:53.640 --> 00:39:57.220
I'm sure you recognize this for what it is, and it was the world's first.

00:40:09.280 --> 00:40:11.120
Everybody went crazy about the idea.

00:40:11.560 --> 00:40:13.180
Everybody, that is, except the craftsmen,

00:40:13.340 --> 00:40:14.920
machines like these would put out of work.

00:40:18.510 --> 00:40:20.230
But 4,000 miles away,

00:40:20.410 --> 00:40:22.310
this system was just what the doctor ordered.

00:40:23.040 --> 00:40:27.960
See, the British were putting the screws on America

00:40:27.960 --> 00:40:29.960
with a naval blockade, and the American...

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:34.180
desperately needed to make their own goods and short of manpower the first

00:40:34.180 --> 00:40:41.190
thing they did was grab that factory system idea. This is the other thing

00:40:41.190 --> 00:40:46.120
Americans were doing at the time, making guns. Take a close look at this rifle.

00:40:47.060 --> 00:40:50.400
This bit here is called the breech block. Now there's a trigger, you pull the trigger

00:40:50.400 --> 00:40:54.220
the spring inside releases this hammer here carrying a bit of flint. The flint

00:40:54.220 --> 00:40:59.260
jumps forward, hits that metal, causes sparks. The sparks cause the powder in

00:40:59.260 --> 00:40:59.980
that little shelf to...

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:04.660
ignite, the flame goes through that tiny hole, ignites the main charge inside the gun, and causes

00:41:04.660 --> 00:41:10.980
the bullet to be fired down the barrel. Complicated bit of workmanship, wouldn't you say? And that, at the

00:41:10.980 --> 00:41:15.270
end of the 18th century, is just what America couldn't do. She didn't have enough skilled men.

00:41:15.920 --> 00:41:21.440
And that put the country in a real hole, because at the time, they were importing most of their guns

00:41:21.440 --> 00:41:26.610
from us Europeans. And half the time, they were on the edge of war with us. So what did they do?

00:41:27.460 --> 00:41:29.980
They came up with a way of making handcrafted weapons.

00:41:30.480 --> 00:41:32.000
without making handcrafted weapons.

00:41:32.200 --> 00:41:34.160
And that's why this isn't what it appears to be.

00:41:34.340 --> 00:41:36.840
See, it's not made by hand, it's made by machine.

00:41:37.980 --> 00:41:39.100
It was a European idea.

00:41:39.440 --> 00:41:42.570
Oh, rather like that Portsmouth block-making system

00:41:42.570 --> 00:41:44.450
that went over like a lead balloon in Britain.

00:41:44.860 --> 00:41:46.100
Only this time it was in France.

00:41:46.300 --> 00:41:48.020
A fellow called Honoré Leblanc,

00:41:48.480 --> 00:41:50.740
who was getting zero interest from his fellow Frenchmen,

00:41:50.980 --> 00:41:54.090
told the American ambassador about his idea, Thomas Jefferson,

00:41:54.290 --> 00:41:57.050
who promptly went bananas, wrote to the folks back home,

00:41:57.110 --> 00:41:58.170
and they jumped at the idea.

00:41:59.080 --> 00:41:59.980
Let me tell you in principle...

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:01.660
what that idea turned out to be.

00:42:03.260 --> 00:42:05.440
It was to get a machine to do all the clever work,

00:42:05.540 --> 00:42:07.100
so you don't need a skilled operator.

00:42:07.780 --> 00:42:08.420
See what's happening?

00:42:09.160 --> 00:42:12.060
The machine cuts only so far and stops.

00:42:19.140 --> 00:42:21.700
So the guy working the machine doesn't need any skill.

00:42:22.170 --> 00:42:24.330
And the reason the machine knows what he doesn't

00:42:24.330 --> 00:42:27.180
is because you use a master of the shape you want to cut

00:42:27.180 --> 00:42:28.000
to set the machine.

00:42:28.390 --> 00:42:29.970
So every time the drill...

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:38.520
and the knives go so far and no further so every time you get an identical piece cut in metal or in

00:42:38.580 --> 00:42:46.650
wood that was Leblanc's idea picked up here in New England by Eli Whitney and John Hall in Simeon North

00:42:46.650 --> 00:42:51.990
to make each part of a gun on a machine set to make that part and that part only over and over

00:42:51.990 --> 00:42:56.570
again now the result of that idea was that if your gun went kablooey in the middle of a battle

00:42:56.570 --> 00:42:59.480
You just got out your little screwdriver and did this.

00:43:04.600 --> 00:43:11.460
And any other block will do because they're standard shape.

00:43:16.380 --> 00:43:19.410
And because they're standard, they're interchangeable.

00:43:22.920 --> 00:43:24.880
And if that doesn't blow your mind, well, it should.

00:43:25.490 --> 00:43:27.830
I mean, almost everything you possess, you only have

00:43:27.830 --> 00:43:29.980
because of the way those guns were made by machine.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:33.500
Have you ever heard of a one-off handcrafted television set?

00:43:34.460 --> 00:43:36.580
So anyway, when these two great ideas got together,

00:43:36.850 --> 00:43:39.850
the factory system, the textile manufacturers were using,

00:43:40.010 --> 00:43:42.530
and the interchangeability idea that the gun makers were into,

00:43:42.930 --> 00:43:44.050
America really took off.

00:43:44.610 --> 00:43:46.210
And look what the country had going for it.

00:43:46.620 --> 00:43:48.020
Water power coming out of your ears,

00:43:48.440 --> 00:43:49.940
mountains of raw materials,

00:43:50.380 --> 00:43:53.460
and a uniquely American way of using machine tools.

00:43:54.680 --> 00:43:56.600
The humble machine tool probably did more

00:43:56.600 --> 00:43:58.480
to make America great than almost anything else,

00:43:58.840 --> 00:43:59.960
because although each machine

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:05.780
tool only did one thing, like grinding or boring or drilling. All machine tools had to solve the

00:44:05.780 --> 00:44:12.900
same general problems, control systems, gearing, power transmission, so that what you learned in

00:44:12.900 --> 00:44:17.140
making machine tools you could use when you made the machines with the machine tools. And what you

00:44:17.140 --> 00:44:23.720
got from that was a kind of technical cascade from guns to sewing machines to bicycles to cars. I

00:44:23.720 --> 00:44:28.120
mean, did you know that Cadillac started with bicycles? Anyway, by the end of the 19th century,

00:44:28.120 --> 00:44:29.440
American machines were so good.

00:44:30.030 --> 00:44:32.060
The only thing that wasn't was the people.

00:44:37.730 --> 00:44:39.790
The fellow who helped to turn people into machines

00:44:39.790 --> 00:44:42.410
used to go berserk watching bricklayers work.

00:44:42.570 --> 00:44:44.280
Or rather, not work.

00:44:45.360 --> 00:44:48.710
He was an American engineer, a guy called Frank Gilbreath,

00:44:48.870 --> 00:44:52.950
who studied expert oyster openers, swordsmen, typists, you name it,

00:44:53.110 --> 00:44:57.050
and got all fussed up about how disorganised ordinary people's work was.

00:44:57.610 --> 00:44:59.970
So he and his psychologist wife set about putting...

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:04.850
things right. They looked at the way people moved when they did a job, put lights on their

00:45:04.850 --> 00:45:11.280
hands, filmed the action, and then used the films to make 3D models of the movement so they

00:45:11.280 --> 00:45:16.240
could redesign the moves so as to make the jobs more efficient. They even put a grid

00:45:16.240 --> 00:45:21.080
everywhere to measure the movement down to the inch. This was world champion typist

00:45:21.080 --> 00:45:29.680
Miss Hortense Stolnitz, showing just what she could do. And you know how the surgeon

00:45:29.680 --> 00:45:29.900
says

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:34.260
forceps and the nurse slaps it into his hand? That was one of the Gilbreaths' ideas too.

00:45:35.080 --> 00:45:39.120
Everywhere they went, they took a job to bits and put it together again in a way that saved

00:45:39.120 --> 00:45:42.290
time. Time and motion was their thing.

00:45:56.350 --> 00:45:59.250
Bored out of your skull at work? Thank the Gilbreaths.

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:04.100
To be fair to them, they ran their own lives the same way, had 12 kids, and wrote a book

00:46:04.100 --> 00:46:05.700
about it called Cheaper by the Dozen.

00:46:06.740 --> 00:46:14.280
So here we are at the modern production line because, one, knowledge of the sky apparently

00:46:14.280 --> 00:46:19.680
cured that Arab car leaf, two, the Arabs got excited by astronomy and translated Greek

00:46:19.680 --> 00:46:24.820
books on it and medical astrology and other science, three, that knowledge helped the

00:46:24.820 --> 00:46:28.780
church to find a way to make alarm clocks that spread and took over people's working

00:46:28.780 --> 00:46:29.260
lives.

00:46:29.620 --> 00:46:29.860
4.

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:34.120
the pendulum that still left sailors looking for a good spring made of steel,

00:46:34.400 --> 00:46:39.740
that five cut screws for precision work, that six built machines to make blocks

00:46:39.740 --> 00:46:43.980
for warships thanks to putting the machines in seven, a long production

00:46:43.980 --> 00:46:48.280
line set up that led to eight, the manufacturing system that helps to give

00:46:48.280 --> 00:46:52.880
us all the same possession. Made by people doing identical things with

00:46:52.880 --> 00:46:56.700
identical machines to make everything from cars to gingerbread men for

00:46:56.700 --> 00:46:58.970
for everybody, not just the privileged few.

00:47:01.280 --> 00:47:04.800
This place, America, is a democracy of common possession.

00:47:05.550 --> 00:47:08.450
And the rest of the industrial world is rapidly going that way too.

00:47:09.480 --> 00:47:10.360
But there's a price.

00:47:11.240 --> 00:47:15.090
The way our lives have to become an extension of the production line.

00:47:17.030 --> 00:47:20.530
We work together, we holiday together, we sit in the same traffic jams together,

00:47:20.710 --> 00:47:22.530
we wear the same clothes, we live in the same house,

00:47:22.880 --> 00:47:25.040
we drive the same car, we have the same ambition.

00:47:27.750 --> 00:47:28.490
That's the price.

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:34.240
the clock and ironically we're back with the question they were asking at the

00:47:34.240 --> 00:47:39.550
beginning of this program what happens to individuality oh sure superficially

00:47:39.550 --> 00:47:43.640
it's there my car is a different color from yours I watch a different television

00:47:43.640 --> 00:47:51.180
program from you but empty your pockets and see what you get pen watch checkbook

00:47:51.180 --> 00:47:59.320
some money credit card keys driving license tone for money lighter

00:48:00.310 --> 00:48:02.330
the paraphernalia of people's private lives.

00:48:03.030 --> 00:48:04.490
And yet, is there one object here?

00:48:04.760 --> 00:48:07.060
that thousands of other people don't own.

00:48:07.800 --> 00:48:08.880
All of it made by machine.

00:48:09.430 --> 00:48:10.270
Not one object.

00:48:10.650 --> 00:48:11.110
uniquely

00:48:11.640 --> 00:48:13.100
individually, me.

00:48:14.260 --> 00:48:15.400
And if I'm not here...

00:48:17.240 --> 00:48:17.780
Where am I?
